Some MCP servers expect you to authenticate with a specific, pre-registered OAuth client instead of relying on Dynamic Client Registration. You can now specify a client ID directly in the oauth section of an MCP server entry in mcp.json:

``json
"my-mcp-server": {
"url": "https://mcp.example.com/mcp",
"type": "http",
"oauth": {
"clientId": "your-client-id"
}
}
`

When a client ID is set, VS Code uses it during the OAuth flow instead of registering a new client, so you can bring your own pre-registered client configuration.

Some MCP servers also require a client secret alongside the public client ID. You can now store a client secret in VS Code's OS-backed secret storage, where it stays encrypted instead of sitting in plaintext config.

When an MCP server entry in mcp.json has an oauth.clientId`, a Set Client Secret CodeLens appears above it.

You can configure temperature and top_p for each Custom Endpoint model, so requests work with providers that have strict parameter requirements.

Add the modelOptions object to a model's JSON configuration:

``json
{
...
"models": [
{
"id": "",
"modelOptions": {
"temperature": 1,
"top_p": null
},
...
}
}
`

Set a property to a number to override the default value that VS Code sends. Set it to null` to omit the parameter from requests and use the model server's default. These options apply to Chat Completions, Responses, and Messages-compatible endpoints.

To require sandboxing in Copilot Agent Host and prevent bypass, use these unified managed settings:

``json
{
"sandbox": {
"enabled": true,
"allowBypass": false
}
}
`

Legacy VS Code sandbox settings and device policies are deprecated. ChatAgentSandboxEnabled` now supplies an overridable default rather than a mandatory requirement in Agent Host. Local behavior is unchanged.

Administrators can more easily attribute Agent Host sessions to users and machines by including operating system usernames and hostnames in VS Code's Agent Host telemetry. Merge this example into your unified managed settings, alongside your existing telemetry exporter configuration:

``json
{
"telemetry": {
"enabled": true,
"capture": {
"identity": true
}
}
}
`

For example, captured identity attributes can include (illustrative values):

`json
{
"user.name": "octokit",
"process.user.name": "devuser",
"host.name": "dev-workstation"
}
`

The OS username (process.user.name) and hostname (host.name) are resource attributes. The signed-in GitHub username (user.name`) appears on runtime spans when supported by the bundled Copilot runtime.

Identity capture is off by default, and the managed value overrides personal preferences. It is independent of content capture. VS Code's host-side control does not govern a runtime's direct exports.

Administrators who need more time to transition to Copilot Agent Host can set vscode.temporaryDefaultToLocalHarness to true in their unified managed settings:

``json
{
"vscode": {
"temporaryDefaultToLocalHarness": true
}
}
`

This keeps the Local harness available and stops automatic switching to Copilot, while preserving explicit, remembered, and current harness choices. Set the managed value to false` or remove it to restore normal routing.
Note: This temporary opt-out also overrides routing required by managed sandbox and permission settings. Local does not enforce those runtime-only rules. Enforcement in Copilot Agent Host sessions is unchanged.

Administrators can permit Assisted while blocking Allow All, giving developers assisted approvals without bypassing permission checks. The Copilot permission picker reflects the modes permitted by your organization's managed settings.

For example, merge this configuration into your unified managed settings:

``json
{
"permissions": {
"disableBypassPermissionsMode": "disable",
"disableAssistedPermissionsMode": false
}
}
`

Use permissions.disableAssistedPermissionsMode to restrict Assisted independently. permissions.defaultMode supplies a starting default, not a restriction on the modes users can select.

The legacy ChatToolsAutoApprove: false policy still blocks both Allow All and Assisted unless you configure explicit managed mode restrictions. Setting only permissions.defaultMode does not replace that legacy restriction.
Note: Assisted Permissions is not currently compatible with permissions.{allow,ask,deny}.

Administrators can generate and refresh HTTP headers for OpenTelemetry (OTel) exports with the new telemetry.headersProvider managed setting. For example, use a helper to supply short-lived authentication tokens for your collector, without asking developers to update headers manually or opt in through personal OTel settings.

This setting applies only to Copilot Agent Host sessions. Configure it in enterprise-managed settings, not user settings:

``json
{
"telemetry": {
"enabled": true,
"endpoint": "https://:4318",
"protocol": "http",
"headersProvider": {
"path": "",
"args": [],
"refreshIntervalMs": 5000
}
}
}
``

The helper writes a JSON object of header names and string values to standard output.

See Configure dynamic headers for helper requirements and further details.

Copilot harness sessions now install or update the agent plugins that your organization requires before using them.

For example, require a plugin in enterprise-managed settings:

``json
{
"enabledPlugins": {
"my-plugin@my-marketplace": true
}
}
``

Plugins enabled by a trusted repository's settings, and plugins your organization assigns to your team, install in the background without blocking chat.
- Managed settings take precedence, followed by trusted repository settings and then your own choices.
- Team assignments apply only when no other choice exists.
- Automatically installed plugins are enabled only for the session and aren't added to your user settings.

Assisted and Allow All remain visible but disabled when restricted by your organization, with an explanation instead of disappearing.

Example Copilot managed settings:

``json
{
"permissions": {
"disableAssistedPermissionsMode": true,
"disableBypassPermissionsMode": "disable"
}
}
``

New chats in the Editor and Agents windows respect your organization's default model, Copilot Fusion tier, and permission level, taking precedence over remembered selections. You can change these defaults within a session where policy allows; unavailable permission modes fall back to Manual.

Example Copilot managed settings, for organizations with Fusion Max available:

``json
{
"model": "fusion",
"fusionTier": "max",
"permissions": {
"defaultMode": "assisted"
}
}
``

When your organization requires an agent plugin, for example with the enabledPlugins managed setting, the Customizations views previously offered Uninstall for it. That action always failed and changed to Retry Uninstall, which also failed. The same happened for the prompts, skills, agents, hooks, and MCP servers that the plugin contributes.

``json
{
"enabledPlugins": {
"my-plugin@my-marketplace": true
}
}
``

The Uninstall action for these plugins and their contributions is now disabled and a message is shown on hover and keyboard focus. This applies to Browse, the plugin details, the plugin editor, and the plugin and MCP server menus.

Previously, when an organization limited MCP to servers from its registry and also defined an allowlist in managed settings, VS Code still required each server to be in the registry. Servers that the allowlist approved but that weren't published to the registry couldn't run.

The enterprise-managed allowedMcpServers list is now authoritative. A server that matches the list can run even if it isn't in the registry, and a registry server that doesn't match is blocked. For example, the following enterprise-managed settings allow one remote server and one local server:

``json
{
"allowedMcpServers": [
{ "serverUrl": "https://mcp.example.com/mcp" },
{ "serverCommand": ["node", "approved-server.js"] }
]
}
`
- An empty list ([]) blocks all servers. If the list is absent, the registry restriction applies as before.
- User and workspace allowlists can't add servers to the enterprise list.
- deniedMcpServers and turning off MCP still take precedence over the allowlist.

Setting: chat.agent.sandbox.credentials.envVars

To protect sensitive information, you can configure which environment variables are masked in sandboxed sessions and hidden from agent tools. Sandboxed tools receive placeholders, and the real values are supplied only to approved HTTPS destinations.

In this example, sandboxing replaces the value of the API_TOKEN environment variable with a placeholder. The real value is restored only in outgoing HTTPS requests to hosts listed in injectHosts, here api.example.com. Requests to other hosts retain the placeholder and don't receive the actual value.

``json
{
"chat.agent.sandbox.credentials.envVars": {
"API_TOKEN": {
"injectHosts": ["api.example.com"]
}
}
}
``

Enter variable names, not secret values, in settings. On Windows, environment variable masking requires local-network access. On macOS and Windows, trust the sandbox CA certificate for tools that require OS certificate trust.

Organizations can direct blocked developers to their own help page by setting communication.support.url in enterprise-managed settings. We recommend pointing this URL to a page where employees can find their organization's Copilot usage policies and support guidance. A valid absolute HTTP or HTTPS URL adds Contact Admin links to supported policy restrictions, such as disabled Agents or organization-disabled models.

For example, in your organization's managed settings:

``json
{
"communication": {
"support": {
"url": "https://ai.example.com/your-companys-ai-landing-support-page"
}
}
}
``

Setting: workbench.sizeCustomizations

Customize panel corners and spacing in the editor window and in Agents windows with the Modern UI enabled. Set corner radii in whole pixels from 0 to 8 and spacing from 0 to 16. For example, you can use square panel corners, add space between panels, and give agent chat panels their own corners:

``json
{
"workbench.sizeCustomizations": {
"panels.cornerRadius": 0,
"panels.spacing": 12,
"agentsWindow.chatPanels.cornerRadius": 4
}
}
``

Compact layouts remain gapless, and the setting doesn't change controls, individual tabs, or native window corners.

Previously, any Copilot enterprise-managed setting disabled the Claude and Codex harnesses, because they don't enforce those settings. An organization couldn't govern Copilot and still offer Claude or Codex to its developers.

Administrators can now permit one or both harnesses by listing them in vscode.allowedHarnesses:

``json
{
"permissions": {
"disableBypassPermissionsMode": "disable"
},
"vscode": {
"allowedHarnesses": ["claude", "codex"]
}
}
`
- The list permits the harnesses it names and blocks the others, so an empty list blocks both. Without the setting, managed settings disable both harnesses, as before.
- Valid entries are claude and codex, in lowercase. Other entries are ignored.
- Permitting a harness only lifts this restriction. Other controls still apply, such as vscode.temporaryAllowLocalHarness: false`.
- Set it in file-based or MDM-managed settings. Server-managed settings don't support it yet.
Note: Permitting a harness doesn't make it enforce your managed settings.

Previously, managed shell permission rules matched only exact text or * wildcards, so a single rule couldn't cover alternatives or word boundaries. In Copilot harness sessions, Shell(...), Bash(...), and PowerShell(...) rules in enterprise-managed settings accept a JavaScript regular expression written as /pattern/flags in permissions.deny, permissions.ask, and permissions.allow.

For example, require approval for commands that start with git push or git pull:

``json
{
"permissions": {
"ask": ["Shell(/^git\\s+(push|pull)\\b/)"]
}
}
``
